          "text": "{\"relation\":\"DIVERGENT\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The blind responses consistently identify faith primarily as trusting reliance on God and his promises, especially trust in Christ for salvation, while recognizing faithfulness, allegiance, and active obedience as established secondary nuances.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book makes faithfulness—obedience sustained by hope in the promise—the central meaning of faith and invokes James’s contrast between living and dead faith.\",\"rationale\":\"The book’s meaning is recognizable within the consensus because every response discusses fidelity, allegiance, or obedience as a legitimate dimension of faith. However, it displaces the dominant definition of trustful reliance and expressly contrasts itself with the commonly taught justification-focused view. It is therefore divergent rather than novel or merely refined.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Habakkuk 2:4 can support faithfulness or steadfast trust, and James 2:17 supports the necessity of works for living faith, but neither verse alone clearly defines faith as “obedience held steady by hope in the promise.” James may describe works as the evidence or completion of faith rather than identify faith itself with obedience.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
